{"id":4666,"date":"2021-12-20T15:53:07","date_gmt":"2021-12-20T23:53:07","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4666"},"modified":"2025-04-16T16:15:10","modified_gmt":"2025-04-16T23:15:10","slug":"3-6-front-range-sensor-1-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-6-front-range-sensor-1-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"3.6: Front Range Sensor"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bc2c0d1\" name=\"6a2213bc2c0d1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bc2c21e\" name=\"6a2213bc2c21e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Introduction to the Front Range Sensor<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The CoDrone EDU is equipped with a<strong> front range sensor<\/strong> that can detect physical objects in front of it. This sensor is the same sensor as the one on the bottom of your drone. The CoDrone EDU library has built-in functions that allow you to detect an object, avoid an object, or keep the same distance from an object. In this lesson, we are going to start by learning how to detect objects in front of our drone.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"wpb_text_column wpb_content_element \">\n<div class=\"wpb_wrapper\">\n<p><strong>Note: The maximum range for the bottom range sensor is 1.5 meters.<\/strong><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Let&#8217;s start by printing out our front range sensor value. We will do that by using the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.get_front_range()<\/code> function. This function will return a value in\u00a0<strong>centimeters<\/strong>. So for example, if you print your front range sensor value, and it is 50, that means that it detects an object 50 centimeters away. Let&#8217;s do an example.<\/p>\n<p>For this exercise, we won&#8217;t be flying our drone. Instead, we will run a program that prints out our front range sensor value once every second. Let&#8217;s use a for loop and print our sensor value 10 times!<\/p>\n<p>Next, we need to use a for loop to print our front range sensor 10 times. We will also <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">time.sleep(1)<\/code> to make sure we&#8217;re only printing 1 value per second.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">for i in range(10):\r\n    print(drone.get_front_range())\r\n    time.sleep(1)<\/pre>\n<p>Try running this program while slowly moving your drone toward or away from an object. This object can be your hand, a wall, a table, or anything big enough for your front range sensor to detect. The limit for the front range sensor is about 150 centimeters, or 1.5 meters.<\/p>\n<p>Here is the full code for the program:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\nimport time\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nfor i in range(10):\r\n    print(drone.get_front_range())\r\n    time.sleep(1)\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bc2c2a7\" name=\"6a2213bc2c2a7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Automating Preflight Checks<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pilots often do a series of pre-flight checks to make sure that everything is working on their aircraft before attempting to fly. One thing we may consider checking is making sure that our takeoff area is clear. The front range sensor on the CoDrone EDU can detect an object up to 1000 millimeters or 100 centimeters (~39 inches) away. Since we have that front range sensor, we can automate this process! For our next program, we will only take off if there is not an object within 500 mm (50 cm) of the front of our drone.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Let&#8217;s start by making a variable called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">distance<\/code>. This variable will hold the range in centimeters that our front sensor will check for. In this example, CoDrone EDU will need 50 cm of clearance for takeoff.<\/p>\n<p><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">distance = 50\u00a0<\/code><\/p>\n<p>Implement a conditional to check if takeoff is clear.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">if drone.detect_wall(distance):\r\n    print(\"Takeoff area is not clear. Shutting down\")<\/pre>\n<p><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.detect_wall(distance)<\/code> will only return true if there is an object within 50 cm from the front of the drone. If that is not true, then we will need to code for the \u201celse\u201d case. If there is no obstacle, the drone will take off and land.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">else:\r\n    drone.takeoff()\r\n    drone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>Here is the full code for this program:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndistance = 50\r\n\r\nif drone.detect_wall(distance):\r\n    print(\"Takeoff area is not clear. Shutting down\")\r\nelse:\r\n    drone.takeoff()\r\n    drone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bc2c32c\" name=\"6a2213bc2c32c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Automated Flight Safety<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that preflight checks are complete, let&#8217;s add in some flight movements! We can use our front range sensor to detect if we are approaching an object. Let&#8217;s add some code in our else case so that we fly forward until our drone spots an obstacle. Since we don&#8217;t know how long it will take for our drone to find an obstacle, we will use an infinite while loop and use <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">break<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><i>\u00a0<\/i>once we spot something. We will also use our <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">move<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">command in short bursts so that we can check for walls more frequently!<\/span><\/p>\n<p>First, we create an infinite while loop, since we do not know how long it will take to find an obstacle.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">while True:<\/pre>\n<p>Next, we will add in our flight movement and conditional check for obstacle detection. Since this while loop is continuously running, if we make our move command execute for a short amount of time, then our conditional check for an obstacle will run more frequently. You could think of this as your drone is closing its eyes for the amount of time you give it a move command. The shorter the drone has its eyes closed, the more frequently it can open its eyes to check for an obstacle.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.set_pitch(30)\r\ndrone.move(0.2)\r\nif drone.detect_wall(distance):\r\n    print(\"Object detected\")\r\n    break<\/pre>\n<p>We also add a <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">break<\/code> at the end of our conditional check to make sure we exit that infinite while loop we made!<\/p>\n<p>Lastly, all we need to do is print a message indicating that an obstacle was found and send the drone a land command.<\/p>\n<p>Here is the completed code for this lesson:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndistance = 50\r\n\r\nif drone.detect_wall(distance):\r\n    print(\"Takeoff area is not clear. Shutting down\")\r\nelse:\r\n    drone.takeoff()\r\n    while True:\r\n        drone.set_pitch(30)\r\n        drone.move(0.2)\r\n        if drone.detect_wall(distance):\r\n            print(\"Object detected\")\r\n            break\r\n    print(\"Obstacle detected.. Landing!\")\r\n    drone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bc2c395\" name=\"6a2213bc2c395\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenges<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Challenge: Drone Basketball<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For this challenge, make your own basketball hoop using a wall and hula-hoop or other materials. Your goal is to have CoDrone EDU go through the hoop after detecting the &#8216;backboard,&#8217; just like a basketball would! In order to complete the challenge, you must do the following:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Create your own basketball hoop for CoDrone EDU to fly through<\/li>\n<li>Your program must have the CoDrone EDU fly towards the hoop, detect the wall\/backboard, and fly through the hoop before landing<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<strong>Challenge: Homing in<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For this challenge, you will be programming your CoDrone EDU to fly as close to the wall as possible! Using the front range sensor, try and write a program that will stop the CoDrone once it reaches a certain distance from the wall; that distance will depend on where you are flying your CoDrone EDU, so make sure to test out different values!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213bc2c3e2\" name=\"6a2213bc2c3e2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned how to use the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s front range sensor to detect how far an object is in front of the CoDrone EDU. This has a variety of uses, ranging from obstacle avoidance to motion detecting![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-6-front-range-sensor-1-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4735,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":""},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[4328,14401,2134,2189],"class_list":["post-4666","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-sensors","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4666","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":41,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4666\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13489,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4666\/revisions\/13489"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4735"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4666"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4666"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4666"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}